     1	# M1 runtime ownership and operations
     2	
     3	This document describes the implemented M1 shared runtime contract. It is for
     4	developers and operators of the generated Relux Tunnel targets; it does not
     5	define end-user copy or promise a future mode.
     6	
     7	## Implementation status and authority
     8	
     9	`ReluxTunnelCore` contains the implemented, provider-owned runtime,
    10	`TunnelProviderAdapter`, strict v1 messages, aggregate diagnostics, and host
    11	projection seams. `ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter` contains the M0-bound production
    12	dependency factory. The deterministic command-line harness composes the same
    13	coordinator and packet-plane factory with controlled dependencies.
    14	
    15	The checked-in generated macOS provider subclass is still a lifecycle shell:
    16	`App/ReluxProxyMacTunnel/Sources/PacketTunnelProvider.swift` does not yet create
    17	`MacOSProviderCompositionRoot`. Consequently, the shared runtime and production
    18	factory are implemented and tested, but the generated provider executable is
    19	not evidence that the live Network Extension invokes them. The iOS production
    20	target is also deferred by ADR-024 and ADR-027. Do not describe either target
    21	as a deployed M1 runtime until its concrete provider entry point is wired and
    22	validated.
    23	
    24	The authority boundary does not change when the host app exits:
    25	
    26	- NetworkExtension owns the system tunnel lifecycle.
    27	- The provider generation owns SSH, TCP, safe DNS, packet-plane, settings,
    28	  cleanup, capability, and diagnostic state.
    29	- `VPNSessionController` owns only host observation, bounded requests, and the
    30	  projection of system status plus provider facts. `retire()` cancels this
    31	  app-side work; it never calls `stopTunnel()`.
    32	- The app may request or display state. It never owns forwarding resources and
    33	  never infers provider capability from app process lifetime.
    34	
    35	The focused lifecycle view is
    36	[`TASK-260715-2rcvr0_m1-runtime-lifecycle.puml`](../diagrams/TASK-260715-2rcvr0_m1-runtime-lifecycle.puml),
    37	with rendered pages under [`diagrams/artefacts/`](../diagrams/artefacts/).
    38	
    39	## Component ownership
    40	
    41	| Component | Public boundary | Internal owner and responsibility | Must not own |
    42	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
    43	| Host controller | `VPNSessionController`, `VPNHostSession` | App-process observation, bounded message requests, stale-snapshot filtering | SSH, packet reads, routes, DNS, or provider shutdown on app retirement |
    44	| Platform roots | `IOSProviderCompositionRoot`, `MacOSProviderCompositionRoot` | Thin NetworkExtension-to-Core adaptation through one shared `TunnelProviderAdapter` contract | A second lifecycle or forwarding policy |
    45	| Provider adapter | `TunnelProviderLifecycle` | One active generation, start/cleanup deadlines, once-only callbacks, request-ID ledger, failure cancellation | UI state or app-process lifetime |
    46	| Runtime factory | `TunnelRuntimeFactory`; production `MacOSProductionDependencyFactory` | Validate the pinned M0 manifest before constructing a fresh graph; bind selected SSH/security and injected network seams | Reusing a consumed generation or bypassing the M0 gate |
    47	| Coordinator | `TunnelRuntime`, `TunnelRuntimeHealthEventSink` | Ordered start, mandatory health, immutable snapshots, reverse cleanup | General UDP or reconnect policy in M1 |
    48	| Packet plane | `M1PacketPlaneFactory`, `M1PacketPlaneSession` | Resource-free prepare followed by post-settings read activation; owns bridge/HEV/read teardown | Network settings authority |
    49	| TCP and DNS | `TCPConsumer`, `DNSConsumer` | Admission and forwarding over the authenticated SSH session; M1 DNS is safe DNS only | Advertising UDP or full mode |
    50	| Settings | `NetworkSettingsPlanBuilder`, `NetworkSettingsApplier` | Provider-owned plan/apply/clear with explicit commit disposition | Host-app route mutation |
    51	| Snapshot stores | `ProviderRuntimeSnapshotSource`, `ProviderDiagnosticsSnapshotSource` | Generation-scoped immutable runtime/capability/diagnostic replies | Free-form labels or secrets |
    52	
    53	## Dependency graph and M0 inputs
    54	
    55	All concrete production dependencies are created per provider generation after
    56	the immutable production-binding gate succeeds.
    57	
    58	| Owner | Dependency | Binding or seam |
    59	| --- | --- | --- |
    60	| `MacOSProductionDependencyFactory` | M0 manifest | [`TASK-260720-1qhxqa_m0-production-bindings.md`](TASK-260720-1qhxqa_m0-production-bindings.md) and `Configuration/TASK-260720-1qhxqa_m0-production-bindings-v1.json` |
    61	| Factory | SSH transport, host policy, credential resolver, error mapper | Root-owned selected libssh2 transport, immutable-profile policy, system-domain Keychain reference lookup, privacy-safe mapping; see [SSH transport](../.spec/ssh-transport.md) and ADR-014 |
    62	| Coordinator | Configuration snapshot | `ConfigurationSnapshotSource`; immutable opaque profile, revision, credential, and trust references |
    63	| Coordinator | TCP / safe DNS | Injected `TCPConsumerFactory` and `DNSConsumerFactory` using the authenticated SSH session |
    64	| Coordinator | Packet plane | `BridgeBackedM1PacketPlaneFactory`, `PacketFlowBridge`, and the accepted [M0 HEV decision](TASK-260715-2jatnd_m0-bridge-hev-decision-adr.md) |
    65	| Coordinator | Settings | Injected plan builder and applier; endpoint exception precedes broad tunnel routes per ADR-020 |
    66	| Adapter | Runtime and diagnostics | `TunnelRuntimeFactory`, `ProviderRuntimeSnapshotSource`, `ProviderDiagnosticsSnapshotSource` |
    67	
    68	The generated-target split and deferred target decisions are recorded in the
    69	[generated project architecture ADR](TASK-260715-32umrc_generated-project-architecture-adr.md).
    70	The canonical decision log is [`.spec/decisions.md`](../.spec/decisions.md),
    71	especially ADR-014, ADR-015, ADR-020, ADR-023, ADR-024, and ADR-029.
    72	
    73	## Startup and teardown
    74	
    75	One `TunnelProviderAdapter` generation has a 60-second start budget and a
    76	10-second cleanup budget. Its coordinator starts in this exact order:
    77	
    78	1. Load and validate one configuration snapshot and its compatible route mode.
    79	2. Authenticate SSH and register its cleanup control.
    80	3. Prepare TCP, safe DNS, and the resource-free packet plane; verify all four
    81	   mandatory components are healthy.
    82	4. Build and apply NetworkExtension settings.
    83	5. Activate packet reads and native packet-plane resources.
    84	6. Recheck mandatory health and publish `connectedDegraded`.
    85	
    86	The successful M1 snapshot has `tcp=true`, `safeDNS=true`, `udp=false`,
    87	`routeMode=compatible`, `routeState=installed`, `routesInstalled=true`, and
    88	`healthy=true`. TCP plus safe DNS is degraded M1 service, not full UDP
    89	capability and not `connectedFull`.
    90	
    91	Startup failure, provider failure, user stop, and system stop converge on one
    92	idempotent reverse cleanup:
    93	
    94	1. Close TCP admission, then DNS admission.
    95	2. Stop packet reads, bridge/native work, and the private packet plane.
    96	3. Clear settings only when apply committed or its disposition is uncertain.
    97	4. Stop DNS, then TCP.
    98	5. Close SSH and release the immutable configuration snapshot.
    99	
   100	An apply failure explicitly described as `notCommitted` does not clear settings.
   101	`committed` and `uncertain` both require clear. If clear fails, the final state
   102	is `failed`, `routeState=clearFailed`, `routesInstalled=true`, and all
   103	forwarding capability facts are false. The adapter completes its stop callback
   104	exactly once; after the cleanup budget it force-closes registered handles and
   105	records a finite cleanup diagnostic.
   106	
   107	`providerDidFail` is also generation-scoped and first-call-wins. It calls the
   108	platform cancellation callback before awaiting cleanup; a later system stop
   109	joins the same cleanup rather than creating another owner.
   110	
   111	## Messages and compatibility
   112	
   113	The frozen legacy discovery request is exactly `{"kind":"version",
   114	"protocolVersion":1}` and is answered independently of provider phase. All
   115	other messages use protocol version 1 and schema version 1.
   116	
   117	| Request kind | Maximum request | Response | Availability and semantics |
   118	| --- | ---: | --- | --- |
   119	| `getProtocolCapabilities` | 4 KiB | `protocolCapabilities`, max 4 KiB | Running generation only; lists the four v1 commands and schema range 1...1 |
   120	| `getRuntimeSnapshot` | 4 KiB | `runtimeSnapshot`, max 16 KiB | Latest lifecycle, route, capability, health, and redacted error facts |
   121	| `getCapabilities` | 4 KiB | `capabilitySnapshot`, max 16 KiB | Independent `tcp`, `safeDNS`, and `udp` facts; there is no synthetic “full” bit |
   122	| `getDiagnostics` | 4 KiB | `diagnosticsSnapshot`, max 64 KiB | Fixed-schema aggregate counters, gauges, histograms, and bounded errors |
   123	
   124	Every v1 command must carry a request UUID. The provider accepts it only while
   125	the same generation is running and only once in the bounded active/recent
   126	ledger. Missing, duplicate, stale-generation, non-running, or retired requests
   127	receive `protocolError/unsupportedValue`. Responses are sorted-key JSON.
   128	
   129	Configuration snapshots are capped at 64 KiB; legacy version, provider
   130	configuration, start request, protocol capability, and protocol error payloads
   131	are capped at 4 KiB. JSON nesting is capped at 16. Duplicate keys, malformed
   132	UTF-8, invalid kinds/values, and unsupported versions fail closed. Known v1
   133	output enums deliberately project unknown lifecycle/route values to `unknown`
   134	and all capability facts to false, allowing additive output evolution without
   135	creating a false-positive capability. Input version, kind, and enumerated-value
   136	discriminators remain strict.
   137	
   138	The detailed codec contract is also available in
   139	[`RuntimeMessages.md`](../Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/ReluxTunnelCore.docc/RuntimeMessages.md).
   140	
   141	## State and capability semantics
   142	
   143	| Coordinator state | Published lifecycle | TCP | Safe DNS | UDP | Route / health meaning |
   144	| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
   145	| `disconnected` | `disconnected` | false | false | false | No usable provider generation |
   146	| `starting(...)` | `connecting` | false | false | false | Partial resources are never advertised |
   147	| `usableTCPDNS` | `connectedDegraded` | true | true | false | Compatible M1 routes installed and mandatory components healthy |
   148	| `stopping` | `disconnecting` | false | false | false | Admission is closing; do not infer availability from remaining handles |
   149	| `failed` | `failed` | false | false | false | Redacted primary error; route state may be `clearFailed` |
   150	
   151	The host combines two authorities: NetworkExtension session status determines
   152	whether the system tunnel is connected, while a newer provider snapshot
   153	determines provider facts. Malformed, timed-out, absent, or stale provider data
   154	means capability is unknown. It is not evidence of `false`, and it is never
   155	reconstructed from app-side cached state.
   156	
   157	## Failure mapping
   158	
   159	### Wire protocol errors
   160	
   161	Every rejected message returns `domain=protocol` and one stable code, with the
   162	supported protocol and schema ranges: `payloadTooLarge`, `invalidUTF8`,
   163	`corruptPayload`, `excessiveNesting`, `duplicateKey`,
   164	`unsupportedProtocolVersion`, `unsupportedSchemaVersion`, `unsupportedKind`,
   165	or `unsupportedValue`. No parser text is returned.
   166	
   167	### Provider callback errors
   168	
   169	The NSError domain is `works.relux.tunnel.provider`.
   170	
   171	| Code | Token | Current M1 production call site |
   172	| ---: | --- | --- |
   173	| 1004 | `lifecycleBusy` | Overlapping start or invalid phase |
   174	| 1005 | `startCancelled` | Stop, retirement, or lost generation during start |
   175	| 1006 | `startupTimedOut` | 60-second start race deadline |
   176	| 1007 | `runtimeStartupFailed` | Runtime factory/start failure |
   177	| 1009 | `internalInvariant` | Exhausted generation or missing adapter at callback boundary |
   178	| 1001–1003, 1008 | configuration/reference/settings vocabulary | Defined for stable platform mapping, but no current production call site emits these enum cases |
   179	
   180	Do not claim an emitted error from enum presence alone. Concrete SSH bootstrap
   181	mapping has its own finite provider-safe codes and stages in
   182	`SSHBootstrapDiagnostics.swift`.
   183	
   184	### Runtime snapshot errors
   185	
   186	The coordinator maps failures to finite domain/token pairs:
   187	
   188	| Boundary | Domain / fallback token |
   189	| --- | --- |
   190	| Configuration load or reference mismatch | `configuration/configuration_invalid` |
   191	| SSH authentication or health | `sshTransport/ssh_session_lost` |
   192	| TCP prepare or health | `tcp/tcp_flow_failed` |
   193	| Safe DNS prepare or health | `dns/dns_upstream_timeout` |
   194	| Packet prepare, activation, or health | `packetPlane/packet_plane_failed` |
   195	| Settings plan / apply / clear | `networkSettings/settings_invalid`, `network_settings_apply_failed`, or `network_settings_clear_failed` |
   196	| Missing internal resource / uncategorized startup | `runtimeInvariant/resource_missing` or `startup_failed` |
   197	| Explicit provider failure stop | `runtimeInvariant/provider_failure` |
   198	
   199	An injected dependency may supply a previously validated redacted error; the
   200	fallback above is used when it does not. The diagnostics store accepts only its
   201	reviewed `RuntimeDiagnosticErrorCode` catalog, at most one error per domain.
   202	Unknown domain/code combinations are discarded and counted, not retained.
   203	
   204	## Diagnostic privacy
   205	
   206	Diagnostics are generation-scoped aggregates. The schema admits only finite
   207	component, counter, gauge, histogram, DNS-result, queue-drop, error, SSH-stage,
   208	and user-action tokens. It has no component-instance, SSH-lane, flow,
   209	association, endpoint, query, packet, or request labels. A response may echo its
   210	top-level request UUID for wire correlation, but that UUID never enters an
   211	aggregate label. The bounded ingestion lane holds at most 256 pending updates;
   212	unknown metric names are rejected and increment the rejected-update counter
   213	without retaining the name.
   214	
   215	Never add raw parser/platform error text, hostnames, IP addresses, ports, DNS
   216	names, packet bytes, profile JSON, credentials, private-key material,
   217	passphrases, host keys, or opaque profile/credential/trust reference values to
   218	diagnostics. SSH context is limited to endpoint family and a reviewed algorithm
   219	token. Redaction tests must exercise the production snapshot call site and
   220	prove prohibited values are absent, including failure output.
   221	
   222	## Troubleshooting
   223	
   224	| Observation | Check | Interpretation / action |
   225	| --- | --- | --- |
   226	| Version reply works but v1 commands return `unsupportedValue` | Provider phase and request UUID reuse | Version discovery is phase-independent; commands require one live running generation and a fresh UUID |
   227	| `connectedDegraded`, TCP and safe DNS true, UDP false | No action for M1 | Expected M1 capability; do not relabel as full mode |
   228	| Startup fails before any consumer call | M0 manifest result and configuration reference | The production factory and configuration load fail closed before graph construction or forwarding |
   229	| Route apply fails | Commit disposition and final route state | Clear only for committed/uncertain; a failed clear remains `clearFailed` and requires operator investigation |
   230	| Mid-session SSH, TCP, DNS, or packet health becomes unhealthy | Runtime error token and cleanup counters | Mandatory health loss transitions through disconnecting to failed and performs the same reverse cleanup |
